{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And he shall not go out of the sanctuary</b> — he does not go after the bier (Sifra here and Sanhedrin 19a). Furthermore, from here our Rabbis learned (Horayot 12b) that a Kohen Gadol (High Priest) may offer sacrifices while he is an אונן (onen, a mourner before burial), and so the verse implies: even if his father or mother has died, he need not leave the sanctuary but rather performs the avodah ( Temple service). <b>And he shall not profane the sanctuary of his Elohim</b> — that he does not thereby profane the avodah if he officiated while an onen, for the verse permitted him to officiate; whereas an ordinary kohen (hedyot) who officiated while an onen — has profaned it. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 21:12).\n\nNow these two midrashim contradict one another. For if the verse permitted the Kohen Gadol, meaning that he need not leave the sanctuary on account of his onenut as would a hedyot, but rather he is able to officiate while an onen — if so, there is no prohibition whatsoever against going after the bier. And likewise, \"he shall not profane the sanctuary of his Elohim,\" according to this reading, serves only to state that his avodah is not profaned, and to teach that a hedyot who officiated while an onen — profaned it. If so, this verse is a negative statement (shelilut), not a prohibition (azharah). Yet the authors of the Halakhot counted it among the prohibitions in Halakhot Pesukot and in Halakhot Gedolot.\n\nFurthermore, I have already written (Ramban on Leviticus 10:6) regarding the baraita that is cited in Torat Kohanim (Shemini Milu'im 42b–43a), where it is explicitly stated that it is a לאו גמור (a complete prohibition), as they taught there: \"And from the door of the Tent of Meeting you shall not go out\" (Leviticus 8:33) — one might think this applies both during the time of avodah and when there is no avodah; therefore Scripture says, \"And he shall not go out of the sanctuary, and he shall not profane\" — when does he not go out and not profane? You must say: during the time of avodah. And they taught: I have only Aaron and his sons, who were anointed with the anointing oil, who if they left during the time of avodah are liable to death; from where do I learn all the kohanim of all generations, etc. So you see it is a לאו גמור, a prohibition that he not leave the avodah and depart. Therefore, in order to uphold all the words of the Sages, I say as follows: the essence of this verse warns the Kohen Gadol that he shall not leave the sanctuary during the time of avodah for any deceased person, and \"he shall not profane the sanctuary\" — that he not abandon his avodah out of honor for the deceased; rather, the honor of the sanctuary and its avodah shall be greater to him than the honor and love for the deceased. All the more so if he abandons the avodah for no reason and leaves, he thereby transgresses this לאו (prohibition).\n\nBut the verse did address the matter of the deceased in order to permit him the avodah while he is an onen, and since it is permitted to him, if he leaves it of his own volition it constitutes profanation of the sanctuary. This is the interpretation of this verse, and I have already written it in the section of Vayehi Bayom HaShemini.\n\nFurthermore, we find that our Rabbis disagreed in the second chapter of tractate Zevachim (Zevachim 16a), and they said: From where do we know that an onen profanes the avodah? As it is written, \"And he shall not go out of the sanctuary, and he shall not profane\" — the inference is: another (i.e., a hedyot) who did leave has profaned. And Rabbi Ilai said: from here — \"Behold, this day they have offered their sin offering and their burnt offering\" (Leviticus 10:19), \"I have brought near\" — by implication, if they had offered it, it would have been valid, etc. Now what is the reason that Rabbi Ilai did not cite \"And he shall not go out of the sanctuary\"? He would answer you: it is written, \"the other who did not leave has profaned.\" But the meaning is to say that since the verse states a prohibition for the Kohen Gadol — that he shall not leave and not profane the avodah by abandoning it — an ordinary kohen is not implied in this sense, that he would profane by officiating. From this there is further proof that the verse is not simply granting permission for the Kohen Gadol to perform the avodah and informing him that his avodah is not profaned thereby, for from this it would necessarily follow that an ordinary kohen does profane it.\n\nNow, on the face of it, it would appear from this according to the first interpretation that it is a negative statement (shelilut) regarding the Kohen Gadol — that he does not profane his avodah, in the language of Rashi. But since the baraita explicitly states that it is a prohibition (azharah), we must say that they reasoned as follows: since the prohibition applies only to the Kohen Gadol, and by implication the hedyot does leave and abandon the avodah — from this we infer that the hedyot's avodah is profaned, for if it were valid, the verse would not have permitted him to abandon it.\n\nAnd so it is cited in Torat Kohanim in this parashah (Sifra, Parashah 2:6): And from where do we know that if he officiated, his avodah is invalid? Scripture says, \"And he shall not profane the sanctuary of his Elohim\" — the inference: an ordinary kohen who officiated — his avodah is disqualified. And the passage cited in Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 19a): \"And he shall not go out of the sanctuary\" — that he does not go after the bier with them, but rather they are concealed and he is revealed, they are revealed and he is concealed — these are the words of Rabbi Meir. And Rabbi Yehudah says: he does not leave the sanctuary at all, as it is said, \"And he shall not go out of the sanctuary.\" And they explained the reasoning of Rabbi Meir: \"from the sanctuary\" — from his sanctity he shall not depart, and since he has recognition, he will not come to touch. And Rabbi Yehudah: along with his grief, perhaps he will stumble and contract impurity.\n\nAll of this is an asmakhta (a scriptural support) of their own making, for they instituted a measure of honor for the Kohen Gadol that he not go after the bier, because he does not become impure for relatives, and a person is distraught over his dead and might touch them, as in the matter of \"And Joseph fell upon his father's face\" (Genesis 50:1); but an ordinary kohen does become impure for his relatives, and for others he is not distraught that he might become impure to them.\n\nAll of this concerning the Kohen Gadol is an enhancement of rabbinic origin (ma'alah midibrehem), and they attached it to this verse in the manner of asmakhtot in the Talmud. And thus I found in the Tosefta in Sanhedrin (Tosefta Sanhedrin 4:1): They said to Rabbi Yehudah: \"And he shall not go out of the sanctuary\" applies only during the time of avodah alone. And there are further proofs in the Yerushalmi there (Yerushalmi Sanhedrin 2:1), but there is no place to cite them here."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The Aramaic/Aramaic-inflected phrases in the Sanhedrin passage (הכירא, אתי למנגע, אגב מררה, דלמא מקרי) are rendered into English directly, preserving the informal halakhic reasoning tone."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:57:54.407632090Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}